$6$-$7$ may appear in all $11$ paths $a_i-a_{i+1}$.

Therefore, $S_p \le 2(2+4+6+8+10)+11 = 2 \cdot 30+11 = 71$. Furthermore $S_p = 71$ iff all the inequalities above become equalities. This is possible only if $\{a_1, a_{12}\} = \{6, 7\}$ (2 cases). For the case $a_1 = 6$, $(a_2, a_4, \dots, a_{10})$ must be a permutation of $\{8, 9, \dots, 12\}$ ($5!$ cases) and $(a_3, a_5, \dots, a_{11})$ must be a permutation of $\{1, 2, \dots, 5\}$ ($5!$ cases), while in the case $a_1 = 7$ it is vice versa. Therefore, the total number of permutations with $S_p = 71$ is $2 \cdot 5! \cdot 5! = 28800$.

b) In order for $p$ to be an optimistic permutation, the number $1$ should be at one of the end positions. Then, the number $2$ should be at one of the end positions of the remaining $p$-sub-permutation of $\{2, 3, \dots, 12\}$, $3$ should be at one of the end positions of the remaining $p$-sub-permutation of $\{3, \dots, 12\}$ and so on. It is easy to check that all such permutations are indeed optimistic. Therefore, their number is $2^{11} = 2048$.

c) Let $p$ be an optimistic permutation and $k \in \{1, 2, \dots, 12\}$ be the position of $12$ in it (i.e., $a_k = 12$). Then, it is trivial to check that $a_i > a_{i-1}$, for all $i = 2, \dots, k$, while $a_j > a_{j+1}$, for all $j = k, \dots, 11$. Thus, $S_p = 12 - a_1 + 12 - a_{12} \le 2 \cdot 12 - 1 - 2 = 21$. Equality is achieved iff $\{a_1, a_{12}\} = \{1, 2\}$. According to b), the total number of such permutations is $2^{11}/2 = 2^{10} = 1024$.

Since $p \mid n + 1$, then $p \mid n + 1 - p$. So, there exists $k \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $n = k p + p - 1$ and $\left\lfloor \frac{n}{p} \right\rfloor = k$. Now, we have
$$
\begin{aligned}
\binom{n}{p} - \left\lfloor \frac{n}{p} \right\rfloor &= \binom{k p + p - 1}{p} - k \\
&= \frac{(k p + p - 1)(k p + p - 2) \dots (k p + 1)(k p)}{p!} - k \\
&= \frac{k(k p + 1)(k p + 2) \dots (k p + p - 1) - k(p - 1)!}{(p - 1)!}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
= \frac{k(k \cdot p \cdot r + (p-1)! ) - k(p-1)!}{(p-1)!} = \frac{k^2 \cdot p \cdot r}{(p-1)!} \in \mathbb{N}
$$
Since $\left(\left[\frac{n}{p}\right], (p-1)!\right) = 1$, then $(p-1)!$ divides $r$ and therefore $\binom{n}{p} - \left[\frac{n}{p}\right]$ is divisible by $p \cdot \left[\frac{n}{p}\right]^2$ as we wanted to prove. $\square$

